cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Clockspeed
message: >-
  If you use Clockspeed, or cite a number it computed, please cite the game
  build it was pinned to. Every quantity this project produces is derived from
  the data files of one specific installation, and a patched build is a
  different derivation rather than a confirmation of the same one.
type: software
authors:
  - name: Clockspeed contributors
repository-code: https://github.com/Remus3/Clockspeed
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A local, deterministic planning and advisory system for the factory-building
  game Satisfactory. Clockspeed reads the data files the player's own
  installation ships with and the player's own save file, computes exact
  production mathematics over them, and issues terse actionable advice about
  what to build next.

  Two properties separate it from the calculators and planners that already
  exist. It carries no dataset of its own: item, recipe and building facts are
  extracted from the install at runtime, so the answer always matches the build
  actually being played rather than whatever version a hand-maintained table
  was last updated against. And the advice path is deterministic end to end,
  with no cloud service and no API key at any point, so every recommendation is
  reproducible from the same inputs and two runs that disagree are a bug rather
  than sampling.

  The solve path uses exact rational arithmetic and asserts the absence of
  floating point. Clockspeed never writes to the game install directory and
  never modifies a save file; saves are copied before they are parsed. Save
  headers carry platform, account and player-name strings in plaintext, so the
  header parser redacts them by default and requires an explicit opt-in to
  render them.

  Not affiliated with, endorsed by or connected to Coffee Stain Studios.
keywords:
  - satisfactory
  - production-planner
  - factory-game
  - save-file-parser
  - deterministic
  - exact-arithmetic
  - read-only
  - game-companion
